    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, I think that math is a bit off. Shouldn't we subtract the salvage value from the initial cost *before* we divide by the useful life? Like, if we don't take the salvage value into account first, aren't we over-depreciating it? Can you show me the formula again with the salvage value factored in correctly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are correct to highlight the issue. To calculate the first year's straight-line depreciation, you first subtract the salvage value from the initial cost, then divide by the useful life.\n\nInitial depreciable cost = Initial cost - Salvage value\nInitial depreciable cost = $85,000 - $5,000 = $80,000\n\nDepreciation for the first year = Initial depreciable cost / Useful life\nDepreciation for the first year = $80,000 / 7 years = $11,429\n\nBy subtracting the salvage value first, you are indeed accurate and consistent.",
